The '''Battle of Tone''' ({{nihongo|Tone-gawa no Tatakai|利根川の戦い}}), fought on October 21, 1600, was the decisive conflict that ended the interregnum following the death of [[Takigawa Kazumasu]] and established the supremacy of the [[Honda clan]] over Mitoyagi. Fought on the fog-shrouded floodplains of the [[Tone River]], it remains the bloodiest single day in Sanese history, known to later generations as the "Field of Broken Swords."

While ultimately a victory for [[Honda Shigetada]], the battle is infamous for its catastrophic scale, strategic chaos caused by multiple simultaneous betrayals, and an unprecedented casualty rate that decimated the warrior caste of an entire generation. Historians describe the engagement not as a tactical masterpiece, but as a "meat grinder" where command structures completely collapsed, dissolving into a massive, confused melee that only ended when the Western Army was physically annihilated against the banks of the swollen river.

==Background==
{{Main|Seto-Tosa Period}}
Following the sudden assassination of the unifier [[Takigawa Kazumasu]] in June 1600, the fragile peace of Mitoyagi shattered. The central administration in [[Okami]] was immediately paralyzed by a power struggle between the "civilian" faction, led by the bureaucrat [[Mashita Morihiro]], and the "military" faction, led by the charismatic general [[Honda Shigetada]].

Mashita, acting as the guardian of Kazumasu's infant heir, accused Shigetada of usurping imperial authority and hoarding gold from the Sado mines. In July 1600, Mashita issued the infamous "Thirteen Charges of Insolence" against Shigetada, declaring him a traitor to the Toyotomi legacy. Shigetada responded by claiming that Mashita was a corrupt official misleading the young heir. 

Throughout the summer, the country fractured. Lords in the west (Yagikoshu and Western Harujima) largely rallied to Mashita, forming the '''Western Army'''. Lords in the east (Eastern Harujima and the Okami plain) rallied to Honda, forming the '''Eastern Army'''. By September, both armies began a slow convergence toward the strategic choke point of the Okami Plain, which controlled the flow of rice and movement between the two halves of the empire.

==Prelude==
On October 19, Honda Shigetada moved his forces to [[Oyama Castle]], securing the high ground north of the Tone River. Simultaneously, the Western Army under Mashita Morihiro and the fierce veteran [[Kiryū Ujimasa]] occupied the southern bank, establishing a fortified encampment at [[Ogaki Castle]].

However, intelligence reached Shigetada that the Western Army intended to cross the river under the cover of night to seize the strategic Sawayama Pass. Abandoning Oyama, Shigetada ordered a forced march in heavy rain. By the early hours of October 21, the Eastern Army had deployed on the northern floodplains of the Tone River, effectively blocking the Western advance. 

Unbeknownst to the Western commanders, Shigetada had engaged in weeks of secret diplomacy. He had extracted a pledge of neutrality from [[Koyama Hidemoto]], a powerful Western general positioned on the crucial tactical height of Mount Tenjin. Conversely, the Western Army had secretly bribed [[Tsutsui Sadatsugu]], a Honda general on the right flank, promising him the governorship of two provinces if he turned his coat.

==Deployment==
On the night of October 20, heavy autumnal rains caused the Tone River to swell, turning the low-lying battleground into a marsh. A thick, freezing fog blanketed the valley by dawn, reducing visibility to less than fifty meters.

* '''The Western Army''' (Takigawa), numbering roughly 78,000, took up defensive positions in a crescent formation ({{nihongo|Gyorin|魚鱗|Fish Scale}}) with the turbulent river at their back. Mashita Morihiro commanded the center behind field fortifications. The fierce warriors of the Kiryū clan, led by Kiryū Ujimasa, held the right flank, while the left was held by the leper general [[Otani Yoshitsugu]]. Crucially, the 15,000 troops of the wavering Koyama clan, led by Koyama Hidemoto, were positioned on Mount Tenjin, overlooking the entire battlefield.

* '''The Eastern Army''' (Honda), numbering roughly 82,000, arrived along the Kōshū Kaidō road. Shigetada deployed his vanguard, commanded by [[Asano Tadamoto]], head-on against the Western center. Shigetada himself commanded the main body and reserves in the rear. The Honda right flank was entrusted to the 10,000 men of the Tsutsui clan.

==The Battle==

===Opening Phase: The Fog of War (06:00 – 10:00)===
The battle began blindly shortly after dawn at 06:00. The dense fog rendered [[arquebus]]es useless due to damp gunpowder and near-zero visibility. The initial clashes were chaotic skirmishes as vanguard units stumbled into one another in the mud. The fighting was characterized by brutal, close-quarters spear and ''naginata'' combat.

The Honda vanguard under Asano Tadamoto pushed aggressively against the Takigawa center, attempting to breach the wooden palisades erected by Mashita Morihiro. However, the mud slowed the Honda advance, turning the center of the field into a quagmire. The fierce [[Shimazu clan]] (fighting for the West) refused to move from their position, claiming they only took orders from their own lord, further adding to the confusion.

By 09:00, the fog began to lift. The Eastern Army's numbers seemed to be prevailing, pushing the Western lines back toward the riverbank.

===The First Betrayal: Tsutsui's Treason (10:00 – 11:30)===
Around 10:00 AM, seeing the Honda vanguard heavily committed in the center and struggling in the mud, [[Tsutsui Sadatsugu]] on the Eastern Army's right flank made his move. Instead of advancing on the enemy, his 10,000-strong arquebusier corps turned 90 degrees and fired a devastating volley into the exposed flank of the neighboring Honda division, the forces of [[Todo Tadamasa]].

The betrayal caused immediate panic. The Honda right flank collapsed inwards. Asano Tadamoto, attempting to rally his crumbling vanguard amidst cries of treason, was struck by a stray arrow in the throat and killed instantly. The Western Army commanders, seeing the Honda lines buckling, ordered a general advance. The Kiryū clan smashed into the collapsing Eastern flank, rolling up the line toward Shigetada’s main encampment.

By 11:30 AM, the situation for Honda Shigetada was critical. His army was shrinking, his vanguard was destroyed, and the enemy was closing in on his command post.

===The Crisis: The Antlered Charge (11:30 – 12:30)===
Realizing his army was on the brink of total rout, Honda Shigetada made a desperate gambit. According to the ''Mitsukoji'', Shigetada remarked to his page, "The tea is cold, and so is my patience." Donning his distinctive antlered helmet ({{nihongo|Kajika-kabuto|鹿角兜}}), he personally led his elite ''Hatamoto'' mounted guard of 2,000 men directly into the advancing Kiryū spear walls. 

This rare act of personal bravery by a commander-in-chief briefly stabilized the center. Witnesses described Shigetada fighting like a demon, his spear ''Tonbo-giri'' claiming dozens of lives. This rallied the remaining Eastern troops to hold their ground in a desperate defensive perimeter.

Simultaneously, Shigetada looked to Mount Tenjin, where the 15,000 troops of the Koyama clan sat immobile, watching the carnage. Needing to force their hand, Shigetada ordered a unit of arquebusiers to move within range of the mountain and fire a volley directly into the Koyama encampment—an act of aggression against his own potential ally.

===The Second Betrayal: The Avalanche (12:30 – 14:00)===
The provocation worked. Panicked by the fire and seeing Shigetada’s desperate resistance, [[Koyama Hidemoto]] made his choice. At 12:15 PM, the massive Koyama host poured down the slopes of Mount Tenjin.

The Western Army raised a cheer, believing their reinforcements had arrived to finish off the Honda. However, Koyama's banners suddenly dipped and his troops crashed into the exposed right rear flank of the Western Army, specifically targeting the unit of [[Otani Yoshitsugu]].

The impact of 15,000 fresh Koyama troops hitting the already engaged Western Army was devastating, but it did not end the battle. Instead, it plunged the field into absolute chaos. 
* The '''Tsutsui''' (fighting for the West) were now trapped inside the '''Honda''' lines.
* The '''Koyama''' (fighting for the East) were crushing the '''Otani''' and '''Kiryū''' (West).
* The '''Honda Center''' was locked in a death struggle with the '''Mashita Center'''.

Command and control disintegrated completely. The battlefield dissolved into a swirling, four-way melee amid the mud. Flags and heraldry became meaningless in the smoke and gore. Accounts from the battle describe a "crimson fog" where soldiers fought whoever was nearest, unable to distinguish friend from foe.

===The Rout and Slaughter (14:00 – Sunset)===
Otani Yoshitsugu, realizing the Koyama betrayal, fought with suicidal bravery. Despite being blind and unable to walk, he commanded his palanquin to be carried into the thickest fighting. Eventually, overrun by Koyama numbers, he asked a retainer to decapitate him and bury his head so the enemy could not claim it.

With Otani dead, the Western flank evaporated. The combined pressure of Shigetada’s resolute center and the Koyama flank attack slowly compressed the Western Army into a tightening pocket against the Tone River.

Around 16:00, the Western line finally broke. Thousands of panicked soldiers fled backwards into the Tone River, which was swollen with rain and running fast. Weighted down by armor, countless men drowned attempting to cross, their bodies forming a grim "dam" that reportedly slowed the river's flow. The Honda forces, enraged by the earlier betrayal of the Tsutsui (who were hunted down and executed to the last man during the rout), gave no quarter. 

The Western commander Mashita Morihiro fled into the mountains but was captured days later. The slaughter continued until nightfall made further pursuit impossible.

==Aftermath==
The Battle of Tone was a decisive strategic victory for Honda Shigetada, but a demographic catastrophe for the nation.

The casualty count was staggering. The Western Army suffered near-total annihilation, with an estimated 58,500 men killed or drowned—a casualty rate of nearly 75%. The victorious Eastern Army fared little better, suffering over 41,000 casualties (50%) due to the initial betrayals and the brutal nature of the close-quarters fighting.

Three days after the battle, Shigetada entered Okami. [[Mashita Morihiro]] and [[Ankokuji Ekei]] were paraded through the streets and beheaded at the dry riverbed of Rokumangawa, close to the current site of Rokumanji. [[Koyama Hidemoto]], despite his decisive intervention, was treated coldly by Shigetada for his hesitation and was given a smaller fief than expected, leading to his eventual obscurity.

The sheer loss of life at Tone traumatized the nation. The military caste of Mitoyagi was so thoroughly depleted that major armed conflict became nearly impossible for the next two decades, initiating the ''Pax Honda''.

==Legacy==
To this day, the floodplain of the Tone River is considered one of the most haunted locations in Okaiken. Local folklore claims that on foggy October mornings, the sound of clashing steel and screams can still be heard. A small shrine, the ''Junkoku-ji'' (Temple of the Martyr Country), stands on the site of Shigetada's command post, where incense is burned annually for the souls of both armies.

The battle is a frequent subject in Sanese ''Jidaigeki'' (period dramas) and literature, often serving as the setting for tragic stories of divided families and loyalty betrayed.

